{%- comment -%}
  Judge.me star badge for a product card.

  Usage: {% render 'review-stars', product: product %}

  Judge.me writes its own markup into `product.metafields.judgeme.badge` and
  the theme just prints it. Nothing is rendered when the metafield is absent,
  which is every product but wander-tee today — Judge.me populates the
  metafield per product, lazily.

  ⚠ The badge ships with `style="display:none"` and Judge.me's own script is
  what reveals it. That script arrives through the **Judge.me app embed**,
  which is NOT enabled in this theme (config/settings_data.json has no app
  embed blocks at all). Until someone turns it on in the theme editor under
  Settings → App embeds, this renders a hidden div and nothing more. That is
  the correct failure mode — never a broken star row — but it is also why
  turning the embed on is a real to-do and not a nicety.
{%- endcomment -%}

{%- comment -%}
  A badge exists for a product with zero reviews too — it carries
  `data-number-of-reviews='0'` and the words "No reviews". On a store that has
  taken no orders yet that is every card, and a grid of "No reviews" reads
  worse than a grid with no star row at all. Show the badge only once it has a
  review to report. The test is on Judge.me's own data attribute, so if the
  markup ever changes the badge simply shows — never disappears.
{%- endcomment -%}
{%- assign jm_badge = product.metafields.judgeme.badge -%}
{%- assign jm_has_reviews = true -%}
{%- if jm_badge == blank or jm_badge contains "data-number-of-reviews='0'" -%}
  {%- assign jm_has_reviews = false -%}
{%- endif -%}

{%- if jm_has_reviews -%}
  {%- comment -%}
    A <span>, not a <div>: this renders inside the card <h2>/<h3>, and a
    heading takes phrasing content only. .jjw-stars sets display:block.
  {%- endcomment -%}
  <span class="jjw-stars">{{ jm_badge }}</span>
{%- endif -%}
